### Comment

#### Suggestion

A `const` keeps its narrowing inside callbacks. The same value imported from another module doesn't:

```ts
// @filename: a.ts
export const user: { name: string } | undefined =
  Math.random() > 0.5 ? { name: "Ann" } : undefined;

// @filename: main.ts
import { user } from "./a";

if (user) {
  user.name;                      // ok
  setTimeout(() => user.name);    // error TS18048: 'user' is possibly 'undefined'
}

const local = user;
if (local) {
  setTimeout(() => local.name);   // ok
}
```

[Bug Workbench](https://www.typescriptlang.org/dev/bug-workbench/?#code/PTAEAEDMEsBsFMB2BDAtvAXKZA6ALgM4BQ8AHgA4D2ATnqAMaWIF0CuB81WA3qCulhbVoiAOagAvqAA+oVogAm8GIngLQAXlABZZHgAWOaskWVUACgCUoAHygADDgCsoAPyhe-TKABEAQUREH0lQLHklFTUAbiIiEAgYBC8sVGQRfGJoVCpaDzkOahDIajNfHGBkHxiiaEhQc3ZOa24iUHzOHC8otp7evv62+MoAa1bQDjwAFSz4SlY8cytNO0bqTrR4S26e+M4SrlAAclXD0GgCUCoCAmgAI1gATyPw5RE1Q6IJWMZmOlhKejIWCadrUGK1er-QGwZpjCbTdBzBZLDR2KFA9boLY7MAjT5EIA)

TypeScript already keeps narrowing in closures for `const` variables and unassigned parameters (#10357), and for closures created after a variable's last assignment (#56908). Imports can be narrowed too (#16658), but in a nested closure that narrowing is lost, even when the import resolves to a `const`. This proposal extends the existing `const` rule to that case. It doesn't ask TypeScript to reason about when callbacks run (#9998).

`const` keeps its narrowing in closures because nothing can reassign it between the check and the call. The same is true of these imports: in an ES module, an import is an immutable binding[^1] to the exporter's binding, a `const` binding is immutable too[^2], and writing to either throws at runtime[^3].

The prototype covers named and renamed imports, default imports of a `const` (`export default user`), and re-export chains, including `export *`. Unchanged:

- `export let`: the exporting module can still reassign it;
- `ns.user` after `import * as ns`: a property access, not an identifier;
- default-exported expressions (`export default (expr)`): they don't resolve to a `const`;
- imported function and class declarations.

#### Motivating Example

Checking an imported constant now works inside callbacks, the same way it does for a local `const`:

```ts
import { logger } from "./logger"; // export const logger: Logger | undefined

if (logger) items.forEach(item => logger.info(item)); // no more "possibly 'undefined'"
```

#### Use Cases

1. Module-level values that may be absent: optional config, feature-flagged services (`export const analytics = enabled ? createAnalytics() : undefined`), DOM lookups. They get checked once and then used in `.map`, `setTimeout` or an event handler.
2. Removing workarounds: today people copy the import into a local `const` or add `!` just to get past the checker. The copy is boilerplate, and `!` also hides a real `undefined` later.
3. Generated code. vue-tsc turns templates into TypeScript, where event handlers are arrow functions inside narrowed blocks, so imported constants started reporting errors there (vuejs/language-tools#6237). The proposed fix works around it in the generated code by unwrapping the reads inside closures (vuejs/language-tools#6238). For imported constants, TypeScript could keep the narrowing itself.

#### Prototype

_The prototype was written with the help of Claude Opus 5.5 and reviewed with GPT-6 Astra._

I tried this in the Go checker (`main` at 8d9b6da99). JavaScript emit is unchanged. The change is one condition in `checkIdentifier` plus a helper ([commit with the change and the test](https://github.com/mcmimik/TypeScript/commit/556f94068)):

```go
func (c *Checker) isConstantVariableOrAliasOfConstantVariable(symbol *ast.Symbol) bool {
	// resolveAlias stops at a re-export merged with a type of the same name, so keep resolving
	// until a symbol has a value meaning of its own
	var seenSymbols collections.Set[*ast.Symbol]
	for ast.IsNonLocalAlias(symbol, ast.SymbolFlagsValue) {
		target := c.resolveAlias(symbol)
		if target.Flags()&ast.SymbolFlagsAlias != 0 {
			if target == symbol || seenSymbols.Has(target) {
				return false
			}
			seenSymbols.Add(target)
		}
		symbol = target
	}
	return c.isConstantVariable(symbol)
}
```

The change leaves the existing compiler baselines unchanged, and benchmarks on Vuetify and vuejs/core showed no statistically significant change in check time. The same check in the JavaScript checker gives identical diagnostics on eight open-source Vue projects (Nuxt, Vuetify, VueUse and others) before and after.

Compatibility: stronger narrowing can introduce diagnostics and change inferred types in previously accepted code, including types in emitted declarations. For example, given an exported `const mode: "a" | "b"`:

```ts
import { mode } from "./a";

if (mode === "a") {
  [1].map(() => mode === "b"); // new TS2367
  const modes = [1].map(() => mode); // now "a"[], previously ("a" | "b")[]
  modes.push("b"); // new TS2345
}
```

These are the same diagnostics the example produces today with a local `const mode` instead of an import. The second one can break otherwise valid code; annotating the array (`const modes: ("a" | "b")[]`) keeps the old type.

CommonJS output is different: an import is read as a property (`a_1.user`), and other code could overwrite it. TypeScript already makes the same assumption in the exporting module: in `a.ts`, `if (user) setTimeout(() => user.name)` compiles to `exports.user` in both places and keeps its narrowing today. The change extends it to the importing side.

If this direction sounds right, I can open a PR with the change and the test.

#### Optional extension

Aliased conditions have the same gap for imports, even without a closure: `const ok = typeof user === "object"; if (ok) user.name` errors, unlike with a local `const`. Applying the same alias check in `isConstantReference` fixes it, and the existing baselines stay unchanged. I can include it in the same PR, or send it separately if you prefer.

[^1]: ECMA-262, [CreateImportBinding](https://tc39.es/ecma262/multipage/executable-code-and-execution-contexts.html#sec-createimportbinding): an import "creates a new initialized immutable indirect binding" that resolves to the exporter's binding.
[^2]: ECMA-262, [InitializeEnvironment for Source Text Module Records](https://tc39.es/ecma262/multipage/ecmascript-language-scripts-and-modules.html#sec-source-text-module-record-initialize-environment): a module-level `const` is created with CreateImmutableBinding.
[^3]: ECMA-262, [SetMutableBinding](https://tc39.es/ecma262/multipage/executable-code-and-execution-contexts.html#sec-declarative-environment-records-setmutablebinding-n-v-s): writing an immutable binding throws a TypeError in strict mode code, and module code is always strict.

